This crate is a translation of BibTeX into Rust: Oren Patashnik's bibtex.web
(BibTeX 0.99e) with TeX Live's change file bibtex.ch, as distributed in TeX
Live 2026 (texk/web2c of the TeX Live source tree,
https://github.com/TeX-Live/texlive-source, tag texlive-2026.1, commit
6a300188053b8f2ded89dbd52293732a706b9c0e). Both files are kept unmodified in
third_party/bibtex/ of the FlashTeX repository
(https://github.com/flash-tex/flashtex); every change is a WEB change file
(changes/flashtex.ch), applied by tools/web2rust as TANGLE applies one.

1. src/generated/ (translated from bibtex.web and the change files)

bibtex.web states its terms:

    This program is copyright (C) 1985, 1988, 2010, 2025 by Oren Patashnik;
    all rights are reserved.

    This program, BibTeX, is available under the same terms as
    Donald Knuth's TeX program.

    (Request to implementors: The WEB system provides for alterations via
    an auxiliary file; the master file should stay intact.)

Knuth's terms for TeX (tex.web): copying is authorized if you make absolutely
no changes to your copy; alterations go in an auxiliary (change) file, and the
master file stays intact. That is how this crate is made: bibtex.web is
unchanged in third_party/bibtex/, and what TeX Live and FlashTeX change is in
change files.

TeX Live's bibtex.ch is in the public domain (TeX Live's web2c change files,
texk/web2c).

2. Everything else in this crate (src/lib.rs, src/system.rs, src/arena.rs,
changes/flashtex.ch, examples/, tests/golden.rs) is FlashTeX's own work under
the MIT licence of the repository (LICENSE at its root).

3. tests/golden/ holds test data: unmodified copies of TeX Live's standard
styles plain.bst and alpha.bst (whose terms allow unmodified copies), of TeX
Live's xampl.bib, renamed variants of those styles damaged by a fuzzer
(mystyle.bst; their terms ask that changed copies be renamed), small styles
and databases the fuzzer generated, and the output of TeX Live's bibtex on
them.

The program prints "This is BibTeX, Version 0.99e (TeX Live 2026)" in its
transcript, as TeX Live's does, because its output is byte-identical to that
program's; whether a product may print that name is for the owner's legal
review (DESIGN.md section 3).
